// Grain Bill M2 question set: questions about Foedrum answered from its documents.
// Answers hold at Foedrum commit 2d64a6e3904c8f514efa135237035c45ae42a57a (origin/main, 2026-11-11).
// Kinds: design (how it works now), why (reason from an ADR), amended (a later ADR changed the rule;
// "stale" is the outdated answer), recipe (what runs, from defaults/), unanswerable (expect a refusal;
// "near" lists the closest passages and why they do not answer).
// Sources: path and line range at that commit; "quote" is a verbatim piece of the range's first line.
// "paraphrased": worded with few words of its source.
// "review": how the entry was checked. "model-triage": drafted by Claude, then checked against its sources
// (with the surrounding text and later amending ADRs) by a separate Claude pass, which corrected 10 entries.
// "hand": also read by a person against its sources.
[
  {
    "id": "design-01",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "Where does Foedrum keep its durable state, and what happens when pushing that state is rejected?",
    "answer": "In the Brewery's git repository: SQLite is written first, and the supervisor's next tick flushes the new events to the state branch. A rejected push stops the engine instead of merging.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/state-branch.md",
        "lines": "3-4",
        "quote": "The Brewery is a git repository, so it also holds Foedrum's durable state"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-02",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "If the Foedrum process crashes and I start it again, what happens to the jobs that were in flight?",
    "answer": "On start the driver expires the attempt leases its earlier run left, since those processes died, so the supervisor's next ticks treat those attempts as lost (one per batch and tick) and no new attempt runs beside them. Its batch leases are not expired, so it carries on driving the batches it held.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/leases.md",
        "lines": "9",
        "quote": "A driver that starts on a store sets the leases its own earlier run left to expire at once"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/leases.md",
        "lines": "18",
        "quote": "Unlike an attempt lease, a batch lease is not expired at a driver's start"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-03",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "How often does Foedrum look at GitHub for new work, and does that eat into my API allowance?",
    "answer": "It polls once a minute. Each read carries the ETag of the previous answer, so an unchanged poll is a 304 that costs no rate limit.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/intake/polling.md",
        "lines": "4",
        "quote": "ADR 0052 sets the polling interval at a minute, which `foedrum serve` applies"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-04",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "What is the open-order limit, what is its default, and which orders count as open?",
    "answer": "`openOrders` (1 to 20, default 3) holds a new order while that many orders are open. An order is open while it has work left, such as a batch Ordered, Brewing, Conditioning or Stuck, so paused, stopped and Stuck orders count, while one with only On tap or cancelled batches does not.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/intake/limits.md",
        "lines": "20-22",
        "quote": "**The open-order limit** (`openOrders`, 1 to 20, default 3)"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-05",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "What is the difference between `foedrum pause` and `foedrum stop`?",
    "answer": "Pause starts no new attempt and lets a running attempt end at its next convenient point, until `continue`. Stop ends an order's work at once: each brewing batch goes Stuck with reason `stopped`, its unfinished attempts are abandoned and the driver stops their processes at its next tick; `retry` continues from the same step.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/humans/pause.md",
        "lines": "3-8",
        "quote": "An engineer halts work with `foedrum pause` and goes on with `foedrum continue`"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/humans/pause.md",
        "lines": "29",
        "quote": "ends an order's work at once, and `retry` continues it from the same step"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/humans/pause.md",
        "lines": "40-41",
        "quote": "Each brewing batch of the order, its sub-orders' too, goes Stuck with reason `stopped`"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "note": "Lines 3-8 include the mermaid diagram that says no new attempt starts and a running attempt ends at its next convenient point.",
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-06",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "If Claude runs out of my subscription allowance halfway through a job, does the job fail?",
    "answer": "No. The engine holds the brewer attempts that limit covers until the window reopens and then goes on by itself; the interrupted step is scheduled again as a retry that does not count against its attempts, so the batch stays brewing. A spend limit or a too-low credit balance, by contrast, makes the batch Stuck.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/usage-window.md",
        "lines": "3",
        "quote": "When claude hits a usage limit of its subscription, the engine holds the brewer attempts"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/usage-window.md",
        "lines": "18",
        "quote": "schedules the step again as a retry; the recipe counts its attempts without it"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/engine/usage-window.md",
        "lines": "29",
        "quote": "A spend limit or a too-low credit balance still makes the batch Stuck"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-07",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "What does the Finer do, and where does it run in the Nip recipe?",
    "answer": "The Finer simplifies a batch's change and commits it before the checks and reviews read it. Nip runs it as the `fine` step between `implement` and `green`.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/recipes/fining.md",
        "lines": "3",
        "quote": "simplifies a batch's change and commits it, before the checks and the reviews read it"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/recipes/fining.md",
        "lines": "12",
        "quote": "Nip runs it as `fine`, between `implement` and `green`"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-08",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "What does a test-first recipe's red proof need from the repository, and what happens if it is missing?",
    "answer": "A test map: `.foedrum/test-map.jsonc` in the target repository, or `test-maps/<owner>/<name>.jsonc` in the Brewery for a repository that sets `\"testMap\": \"brewery\"`. Without one the batch stops Stuck (`environment`) at its start, naming the fix, before any brewer runs, unless the recipe runs a survey and the repository keeps its own map, in which case the survey proposes one.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/checks/red-proof.md",
        "lines": "10-11",
        "quote": "**A red proof needs a test map**"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/checks/red-proof.md",
        "lines": "15",
        "quote": "Where the recipe runs a survey check and the repository keeps its map, the survey proposes one"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/glossary.md",
        "lines": "31",
        "quote": "`.foedrum/test-map.jsonc` in a target repository, or the Brewery's `test-maps/<owner>/<name>.jsonc`"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "note": "Line 12 covers the Brewery-kept map, line 13 the repository-kept map without a survey; line 15 is the survey exception.",
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-09",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "When Foedrum runs tests that an AI wrote, can that code reach the internet or my home folder?",
    "answer": "On Linux, WSL2 and macOS a check runs in Foedrum's own OS sandbox (bwrap or sandbox-exec) with no network, the home folder hidden and writes only in the worktree and a temporary folder; if the sandbox cannot start, the check is not run. On native Windows checks run unsandboxed, and each reading says so.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/checks/sandbox.md",
        "lines": "3-4",
        "quote": "A check's command line runs in an OS sandbox of Foedrum's own"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-10",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "Will my laptop doze off while Foedrum is in the middle of a job?",
    "answer": "Not on its idle timer: `foedrum brew` and `foedrum serve` hold off idle sleep while an attempt runs and let go a minute after the last one ends. The display may still turn off, and the machine still sleeps when you ask, close the lid or the battery is critical; `\"keepAwake\": false` turns the hold off. The hold needs a helper (PowerShell on Windows, `caffeinate` on macOS, `systemd-inhibit` on Linux where systemd runs); where the helper is missing, the machine sleeps as before.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/platform/keep-awake.md",
        "lines": "3-6",
        "quote": "`foedrum brew` and `foedrum serve` keep the machine from sleeping on its idle timer"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/platform/keep-awake.md",
        "lines": "14-15",
        "quote": "| Linux | `systemd-inhibit --what=idle --who=foedrum"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-11",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "How are the engine's invariants tested, and how do I reproduce a failing run?",
    "answer": "Property tests generate seeded event sequences and engine scenarios, including what a fake brewer runner does in each attempt (hangs, lost attempts, stalls, denial storms), and run them through `decide`, the guard, the fold and the supervisor on an in-memory store, one property per invariant I1 to I6. A failure is shrunk and reported with its seed; setting `FOEDRUM_SEED` to that seed replays it.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/design/testing/property.md",
        "lines": "3-6",
        "quote": "on the engine: generated sequences must keep invariants I1"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/testing/fake-runner.md",
        "lines": "3",
        "quote": "with scripted behaviours: succeed, fail (retryable or not), hang, crash mid-step"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "design-12",
    "kind": "design",
    "question": "Which part of the system is responsible for stopping an agent from touching files, network or credentials outside the folder it works in?",
    "answer": "Claude Code's sandbox, together with the per-attempt settings Foedrum writes (tool allow and deny rules, `--setting-sources \"\"`, a minimal environment). Those settings allow file tools only inside the worktree, so a call outside it, through `..` or through a link that leads outside, is denied.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/trust-boundaries.md",
        "lines": "9",
        "quote": "A brewer reaching outside its worktree: files, network, credentials"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/design/security.md",
        "lines": "31",
        "quote": "Generated per-attempt settings allow file tools only inside the worktree"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-01",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why is Foedrum written in TypeScript rather than plain JavaScript or Python?",
    "answer": "Foedrum's core is a state machine whose correctness depends on handling every state and step outcome, and TypeScript's discriminated unions with an exhaustive switch turn a forgotten state or outcome into a compile error, which plain JavaScript cannot check. Python was judged weaker for a cross-platform CLI with a live web dashboard and would mean two runtimes for users.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0001-typescript-on-node.md",
        "lines": "6-6",
        "quote": "Foedrum's core is a state machine whose correctness depends on handling every state and every step outcome."
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0001-typescript-on-node.md",
        "lines": "16-16",
        "quote": "Discriminated unions plus exhaustive `switch` with `never` turn a forgotten state or outcome into a compile error."
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0001-typescript-on-node.md",
        "lines": "21-22",
        "quote": "no compile-time exhaustiveness checks; the main stability tool is lost."
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-02",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why is the code under a license that makes people share their changes, and why do contributors have to sign an agreement?",
    "answer": "The project wants to be genuine open source that individuals and companies can use, while preventing closed commercial resale, in particular running a modified Foedrum as a hosted service without sharing the changes; hence the AGPL-3.0. The Contributor License Agreement lets the project later relicense or offer a commercial licence alongside the AGPL.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0009-agpl-and-cla.md",
        "lines": "6-6",
        "quote": "while preventing closed commercial resale, in particular offering a modified Foedrum as a hosted service without sharing the changes"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0009-agpl-and-cla.md",
        "lines": "10-10",
        "quote": "so the project can later relicense or offer a commercial licence alongside the AGPL"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-03",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why does the process runner kill a process that writes too much output instead of just keeping part of it?",
    "answer": "The runner held all output without a limit, and commands run on untrusted repository content can write without end; a measured test made it throw `RangeError: Invalid string length`, an uncaught exception that would crash the engine, the gate or the cleanup while the writer kept running. Killing at a 4 MiB limit bounds memory and stops a runaway writer at once, and because a killed process has no exit code a caller never mistakes cut output for complete output, which keeping only the first or last bytes would risk.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0035-the-process-runner-kills-at-an-output-limit.md",
        "lines": "8-8",
        "quote": "Commands run against untrusted repository content can write far more than any caller needs before their deadline"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0035-the-process-runner-kills-at-an-output-limit.md",
        "lines": "19-20",
        "quote": "The runner's memory is bounded for every caller, with or without a deadline"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0035-the-process-runner-kills-at-an-output-limit.md",
        "lines": "25-26",
        "quote": "a caller that parses stdout (`git worktree list --porcelain`, JSON) would read a cut-off text that looks whole"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-04",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why don't the commits the AI agents make carry a line crediting the AI tool that wrote them?",
    "answer": "A brewer's commits land in a target repository whose history belongs to the team that owns it, and Foedrum stays neutral about the tools behind it; leaving Claude Code's default would make every commit advertise a tool the repository's owner did not choose. So the runner's settings always switch attribution off, using empty strings rather than `false` because older Claude Code versions reject `false` and skip the whole settings file, deny rules included.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0151-brewers-write-no-attribution.md",
        "lines": "6-6",
        "quote": "A brewer's commits land in a target repository, whose history belongs to the team that owns it, and Foedrum stays neutral about the tools behind it"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0151-brewers-write-no-attribution.md",
        "lines": "12-12",
        "quote": "says an earlier version rejects it and skips the whole settings file that holds it"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0151-brewers-write-no-attribution.md",
        "lines": "21-21",
        "quote": "Every commit in a target repository would advertise the tool, which the repository's owner did not choose."
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-05",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why do brewers run claude with its auto-updater and nonessential traffic disabled?",
    "answer": "During a brew, claude updated itself from 2.1.301 to 2.1.310 mid-brew, because brewers read no user settings; the binary is the user's own, so that update changed the claude every session on the machine runs and the version Foedrum's stream contract was checked against. Nonessential traffic is also turned off because telemetry, error reports and feature-flag fetching are not needed by an attempt and can change claude's behaviour between attempts of one brew.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0177-brewers-run-without-claude-s-auto-updater-and-nonessential-traffic.md",
        "lines": "6",
        "quote": "A brewer's claude starts with `--setting-sources \"\"`"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0177-brewers-run-without-claude-s-auto-updater-and-nonessential-traffic.md",
        "lines": "9-10",
        "quote": "In the fourth brew (batch `9-1`, #442)"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0177-brewers-run-without-claude-s-auto-updater-and-nonessential-traffic.md",
        "lines": "25",
        "quote": "| `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC` | `1` | The updater again"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0177-brewers-run-without-claude-s-auto-updater-and-nonessential-traffic.md",
        "lines": "48",
        "quote": "**`DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER` alone.** It stops the update that was seen, but leaves telemetry"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-06",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "My machine has an older git and Foedrum refuses to start; why does it insist on a newer one?",
    "answer": "Foedrum requires git 2.38 because the check of open pull requests against their moved base runs `git merge-tree --write-tree`, which arrived in git 2.38; before, that check was silently skipped on older git. A check that runs on some machines and silently not on others is a gap in what an open pull request is told, and git 2.38 is four years old.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0200-foedrum-requires-git-2-38.md",
        "lines": "7-7",
        "quote": "runs `git merge-tree --write-tree`, which came in git 2.38 (October 2022)"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0200-foedrum-requires-git-2-38.md",
        "lines": "9-9",
        "quote": "a check that runs on some machines and silently not on others is a gap in what an open pull request is told, and git 2.38 is four years old"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-07",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why are the design docs written with a line break after every sentence, even mid-paragraph?",
    "answer": "Git merges line by line, so two pull requests that change different sentences of one long paragraph line always conflict; the old architecture.md, one paragraph per line, caused most of a day's merge conflicts. With one sentence per line the rendered page is the same and git merges edits to different sentences.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0149-docs-layout-a-map-and-one-page-per-concept.md",
        "lines": "7-9",
        "quote": "Most of it was written one paragraph per line"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0149-docs-layout-a-map-and-one-page-per-concept.md",
        "lines": "22-23",
        "quote": "**One sentence per line** in every page (semantic line breaks)."
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "note": "Replaced a draft that duplicated amended-10 (ADR 0263).",
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-08",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why does a public pull request not name its sibling pull request in a private repository?",
    "answer": "GitHub hides a private pull request's contents, but naming it in a public body would still reveal the private repository's name and that it has a pull request of this change. So a sibling is named only where everyone who reads this pull request can read it too; a hidden sibling is still listed by its place in the merge order, so the reviewer sees no gap.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0300-a-public-pull-request-names-a-sibling-in-a-private-repository-by-its-place-only.md",
        "lines": "8",
        "quote": "GitHub hides that pull request's contents from outsiders, but the repository's name"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0300-a-public-pull-request-names-a-sibling-in-a-private-repository-by-its-place-only.md",
        "lines": "20",
        "quote": "**A sibling is named only where everyone who reads this pull request can read it too.**"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0300-a-public-pull-request-names-a-sibling-in-a-private-repository-by-its-place-only.md",
        "lines": "31",
        "quote": "A hidden sibling's line reads `<n>. A pull request in a private repository: <state>, <when>.`"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0300-a-public-pull-request-names-a-sibling-in-a-private-repository-by-its-place-only.md",
        "lines": "53",
        "quote": "**Leave a private sibling out of the section**: the public pull request would show a merge order with a gap"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-09",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why can't I start work on the same issue again while its change is still waiting to be merged?",
    "answer": "An issue's number means its latest batch, and allowing a new order while the pull request was open let a failed check send the older batch back to brewing behind the later one; in one run a command brewed the wrong batch and reported success. Refusing the new order keeps one live batch per issue, so its number always reaches it; to order again, close the pull request first.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0339-an-issue-has-one-live-batch-no-second-batch-while-its-pull-request-is-open.md",
        "lines": "6-7",
        "quote": "A number means its issue's latest batch"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0339-an-issue-has-one-live-batch-no-second-batch-while-its-pull-request-is-open.md",
        "lines": "11-12",
        "quote": "brewed the later batch to on tap and exited 0, with the older batch still Stuck"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0339-an-issue-has-one-live-batch-no-second-batch-while-its-pull-request-is-open.md",
        "lines": "23-23",
        "quote": "Then an issue has one live batch, so its number always reaches it"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0339-an-issue-has-one-live-batch-no-second-batch-while-its-pull-request-is-open.md",
        "lines": "51-51",
        "quote": "A person who wants a second batch closes the pull request first."
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "why-10",
    "kind": "why",
    "question": "Why must every recipe declare a required type, mash or brew?",
    "answer": "Planning recipes used to be a fixed list of three names, so a Brewery's own planning recipe under another name was neither refused for a sub-order nor counted as planning, and the Innkeeper told planning from building only by descriptions. The type is required rather than optional with a default of `brew`, because a default would let a planning recipe silently count as building.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0365-a-recipe-has-a-type-mash-or-brew.md",
        "lines": "7-9",
        "quote": "was a list of three names, `PLANNING_RECIPES` in `src/schemas/plan.ts`"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0365-a-recipe-has-a-type-mash-or-brew.md",
        "lines": "54-54",
        "quote": "a Brewery's planning recipe would silently count as building, the gap this record closes"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "amended-01",
    "kind": "amended",
    "question": "Can I put comments and trailing commas in the frontmatter of a brewer file or an out doc?",
    "answer": "Yes. All frontmatter (brewers, out docs, house-yeast rules) is read with the same JSONC reader as configuration, so comments and trailing commas are allowed; strictness comes from validating each frontmatter object against its schema.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0020-jsonc-frontmatter.md",
        "lines": "11-12",
        "quote": "All frontmatter (brewers, out docs, house-yeast rules) is read with the same JSONC reader as configuration"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0007-jsonc-config-and-json-frontmatter.md",
        "lines": "10",
        "quote": "are Markdown with **JSON frontmatter** between `---` lines"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": false,
