    "id": "amended-05",
    "kind": "amended",
    "question": "When Foedrum runs a repository's test command, can that command reach the internet?",
    "answer": "On Linux, WSL2 and macOS a check's command runs in an OS sandbox of Foedrum's own (bubblewrap or Seatbelt) with no network, and it does not run at all if the sandbox cannot start. Native Windows has no such sandbox, so there it runs unsandboxed and each reading says `\"unsandboxed\": true`.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0076-check-steps-run-in-an-os-sandbox-with-no-network.md",
        "lines": "28-29",
        "quote": "**Never unsandboxed outside Windows.**"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0076-check-steps-run-in-an-os-sandbox-with-no-network.md",
        "lines": "38",
        "quote": "No network: a namespace of its own with no interface to the host's"
      },
      {
        "path": "docs/adr/0069-check-steps-run-the-repositorys-commands-in-an-attempt-of-their-own.md",
        "lines": "18",
        "quote": "**Checks run outside the OS sandbox, for now.**"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "stale": "ADR 0069: checks run outside the OS sandbox, as the Cellar's user, with network and home-folder access.",
    "note": "ADR 0140 later adds a repository `setup` command (e.g. npm ci) that runs before the checks with the network (0140 lines 12 and 22: a check step has no network; setup has). ADR 0158 then put setup into the check sandbox with the network kept, so it is unsandboxed only on native Windows; ADR 0218 gives a Linux setup its own network namespace through slirp4netns or pasta. The checks themselves stay without network.",
    "review": "hand"
  },
  {
    "id": "amended-06",
