  },
  {
    "id": "recipe-07",
    "kind": "recipe",
    "question": "If a new test needs a function that doesn't exist yet, what should the placeholder do?",
    "answer": "The Gauger, which writes the failing tests first, adds the smallest stub that lets the tests load and makes it throw an error with the message `not implemented`. It must never return a value such as false, 0, [] or null, because a test that passes against it fails the red proof.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "path": "defaults/brewers/gauger.md",
        "lines": "35",
        "quote": "**Stubs throw `not implemented`.**"
      },
      {
        "path": "defaults/recipes/nip.jsonc",
        "lines": "29",
        "quote": "{ \"id\": \"gauge\", \"brewer\": \"gauger\", \"when\": \"recipe.testFirst\" },"
      }
    ],
    "paraphrased": true,
    "review": "model-triage"
  },
  {
    "id": "recipe-08",
    "kind": "recipe",
    "question": "In a quick fix, if the code stops compiling or tests break after the fix is written, where does the work go?",
    "answer": "The green step runs build, typecheck, lint, format and test; a failure goes back to Cellarman (the implement step), whichever step broke it, at most once, and the Finer then runs again.",
